    <summary>Offline powerpoint password remover for your own .pptx files: it recovers a forgotten open password with a wordlist and rule-based mutations, across a whole folder at once, so you can unlock PowerPoint decks and remove password protection.</summary>
    <description>PowerPoint Password Remover Tool is a commercial Windows program and local powerpoint password remover for .pptx files you own. It targets one problem: password protection on a presentation whose open password nobody remembers. For a few office PCs or a managed fleet it is a small package that keeps the whole job on the machine where the file sits.

![Password Remover Tool for PowerPoint screenshot 1](https://download.softorbits.com/files/pkg-img/ppt/ppt-v1-main.png)

## What the package installs

The package puts the free trial of a commercial powerpoint password remover on the PC, and working with it beyond the trial requires buying a license. A search engine and a starter wordlist ship inside the installer, so the first launch needs no further download.

## How to use PowerPoint Password Remover Tool

1. Start the program and drop your locked .pptx files on the window, or choose Add Files or Add Folder.
2. Press Start. The program reads each encryption header and selects the Office scheme it belongs to.
3. Follow the status of every file in its own row, with live speed and the rule set in use.
4. When a password appears, copy it.
5. Open the original presentation in PowerPoint and type the password there.

Trial note: the free trial performs the whole recovery and shows the outcome in a preview popup, while the complete password stays behind a Buy banner. A license lifts that.

![Password Remover Tool for PowerPoint screenshot 2](https://download.softorbits.com/files/pkg-img/ppt/ppt-v1-found.png)

## Who needs to unlock PowerPoint decks on managed PCs

- Help desk teams that receive requests to unlock powerpoint decks whose owner has left or forgotten the password.
- Offices with archived sales and pitch decks that someone chose to password protect years ago.
- Admins who want recovery to stay inside the company network and not on a third-party server.

## How the search works

The program starts with a wordlist of likely passwords. When the list is exhausted it applies rule-based mutations that mirror common habits, such as a name followed by digits. The document is never resaved, so the file on disk stays exactly as it was. A graphics card speeds up modern Office files, and the processor takes over when no card is present.

![Password Remover Tool for PowerPoint screenshot 3](https://download.softorbits.com/files/pkg-img/ppt/ppt-v1-searching.png)

## Limits to know before you remove password from PowerPoint decks

The tool is meant for decks you created or may open. PowerPoint 2013 and later encrypt files with AES-256 and a deliberately slow key step. Names, words and short number endings are realistic targets, and a long random password is not. The current version reads .pptx only, so a legacy .ppt from PowerPoint 2003 is outside its reach.

## Trial and license

Chocolatey installs the trial build only. Seeing the password takes a license from SoftOrbits, sold as lifetime, 1-year or business plans for several PCs. Details sit on the [Password Remover Tool for PowerPoint](https://www.softorbits.net/powerpoint-password-recovery/?utm_source=chocolatey&amp;utm_medium=package) page.

## FAQ

### How can I remove password protection from a PPT file?

Press Start with the file loaded and wait for the result row. The password it reports opens the original deck in PowerPoint, where you can remove protection under File &gt; Info.

### Is there a way to remove a PowerPoint password online for free?

Web pages exist, yet they take a copy of your slides. This program runs offline on your PC, and the trial performs the full search without any upload.

### How do I remove a forgotten password from a PPT file?

Add the .pptx and start the search. Short and word-based passwords return in reasonable time, while a long random one on a recent PowerPoint may not.
